# pacman's own hooks run systemd-sysusers, systemd-tmpfiles and daemon-reload. Enabling a unit is
# the administrator's call, not a package's.

post_install() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-speech is installed. Two things before this host can hear or speak:

    1. Fetch the models (813MB, once — they live in /var/lib/kgsm-speech/models and survive
       every upgrade):

         kgsm-speech-fetch-models

    2. Enable the SOCKET, not the service:

         systemctl enable --now kgsm-speech.socket

  The service is meant to sit inactive. It wakes on the first connection and exits again after
  Speech__IdleMinutes — that is the only thing that returns the ~1.6GB and ~1.1GB of video memory
  the models cost, so an inactive kgsm-speech.service is this leaf working correctly.

  Check it with:  kgsm-speech --voices     (activates the daemon; loads no model)

  Run that as root or as a member of the kgsm group — the socket is 0660 kgsm:kgsm, and those
  permissions are this daemon's whole access boundary.

  Optional overrides live in /etc/kgsm-speech/kgsm-speech.env. Without a usable GPU both
  recognition and synthesis fall back to the processor rather than refusing.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  Re-activate to pick up the new build — the daemon holds the old binary until it exits:

    systemctl stop kgsm-speech.socket kgsm-speech.service
    systemctl start kgsm-speech.socket

MSG
}
